    "abstract": "The concept of UAM-friendly neighborhoods has emerged as a promising strategy for supporting Urban Air Mobility (UAM) deployment. However, little is known about how residents evaluate neighborhood renewal plans that incorporate UAM infrastructure, particularly when noise impacts are considered. This study examines residents’ preferences for UAM-friendly neighborhood renewal using stated preference survey data and a mixed logit model. Results show that drone docking pad installation costs, vertiport proximity, social influence, property management fees, and noise exposure significantly affect residents’ choices. Younger, higher-income, and technology-oriented residents are more supportive of UAM integration, whereas highly educated residents and those living on lower floors (1–7) are more sensitive to noise impacts. These findings provide quantitative evidence on the trade-offs residents make between UAM infrastructure benefits and neighborhood noise exposure, offering insights for planning socially acceptable UAM-friendly communities.",
