  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on stated preference survey data rather than observed behavior in an active Urban Air Mobility deployment.",
      "what": "This study analyzed resident preferences for Urban Air Mobility (UAM) neighborhood integration using stated preference survey data and a mixed logit model in Guangzhou, China. It measured how choices are influenced by drone docking pad installation costs, vertiport proximity, social influence, property management fees, and noise exposure. The analysis reveals that younger, wealthier, and tech-oriented residents favor UAM integration, while highly educated individuals and those living on floors 1 to 7 show the highest sensitivity to noise.",
      "why": "It provides quantitative evidence on how residents trade off the convenience of aerial transit infrastructure against localized noise pollution, establishing which demographic groups and building levels require targeted mitigation during neighborhood renewal."
